WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.540
In 2003, the Human Genome Project finished
a monumental scientific achievement:

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:09.460
the first complete human genome map.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:13.059
A couple of decades later, and you can get
a genetic test done

00:00:13.059 --> 00:00:16.930
by spitting in a tube and paying
a couple of hundred dollars.

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:19.830
But that's not quite
what I'm here to talk about.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:24.050
This is CUBRIC, one of the most advanced
brain imaging centres in Europe,

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:30.900
and here they’re not scanning genomes:
they’re scanning connectomes.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.329
The 'connectome' is a phrase that was coined
several years ago

00:00:35.329 --> 00:00:37.460
and it's to really encapsulate the idea of

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:40.120
mapping all the connections in the brain,
in one go.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.210
Those connections are formed by what we call
the white matter in the brain.

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:44.910
So we know the brain has grey matter,

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:45.920
it also has white matter,

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:47.239
and these form these wires.

00:00:47.239 --> 00:00:50.260
We're also really interested in what we call
the microstructure,

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.760
being able to differentiate between
the size and shapes of cells

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.580
that may be only
a thousand of a millimetre across.

00:00:56.589 --> 00:00:58.249
What this scanner will enable us to do

00:00:58.249 --> 00:01:01.589
is to get information about differences across
the whole brain

00:01:01.589 --> 00:01:04.600
in a way that would only previously be possible
post-mortem.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:06.740
Now, I’d be getting a scan myself, but alas,

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:09.680
I have a bit too much metal in my body.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.070
Don't worry about me or the camera getting
sucked into this, though,

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:13.860
this is just the simulator
that they train on.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.050
There's no actual magnets in there.

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:17.990
The real scanner is over there.

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:20.590
This MRI scanner,
the magnetic field gradient allows you

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:23.250
to be sensitive to the motion of water molecules

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:26.210
over a distance of about
a thousandth of a millimetre.

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:28.210
To map somebody's complete connectome,

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:30.130
first we have to acquire the raw data,

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:32.210
then we have to fit it to mathematical models,

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:34.330
then we have to reconstruct those pathways.

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:38.020
And so we rapidly expand from
a few hundreds of megabytes

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:39.800
up to tens of gigabytes.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:43.930
All the hopeful science fiction fans out there
shouldn’t get too excited:

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:47.990
in the same way as copying someone’s genome
doesn't let you make an actual copy of them,

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:52.150
just a baby that’s an identical twin and
that doesn't know anything,

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:56.750
scanning a connectome doesn’t mean that
you can suddenly upload someone into a computer.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:02:00.730
Scanners like this generate
road maps, not satellite photos.

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:01.900
Heck of a road map, though.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:03.490
I think we're a long way from being able

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:07.060
to identify differences in personality at
the present time,

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:09.119
but that's not to say we won't be able to
do it in the future.

00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:11.129
Generally, people are connected
in the same ways,

00:02:11.129 --> 00:02:13.680
but it's subtle differences in the shapes
of the tracks

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.069
that does seem to be highly reproducible.

00:02:15.069 --> 00:02:20.650
If we scan 300 people, and then scan one of
those people just one more time,

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:23.769
you can easily identify them
out of that 300 people.

00:02:23.769 --> 00:02:27.810
Essentially, any neurological or psychiatric
disease that you can think of,

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:30.540
they're all symptoms of
malfunction of the brain.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.049
And we know that function of the brain relies
on information transfer

00:02:34.049 --> 00:02:35.530
from one region to another.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:40.140
We can say things such as "there's something
different with the integrity of the white matter".

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:43.360
Potentially, if you can name a neurological
or psychiatric disease,

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.200
the information that we get from this scanner

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.819
is going to be a boon
and give us major new insights.

00:02:47.819 --> 00:02:50.060
And if we could bring
the cost of the technology down,

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:52.739
you could go in for your connectome scan,

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:57.200
and have the personalised treatment tailored
to your specific connectomic deficit.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:00.290
Right now connectome scans are cumbersome
and expensive,

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:03.430
and they take a long time to process and visualise.

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:07.189
But that was true of genome scans
just 20 years ago.

00:03:07.189 --> 00:03:12.720
In a decade or two, connectome research might
unlock just as many benefits for the world.

00:03:14.249 --> 00:03:17.180
Thank you very much to everyone at CUBRIC,
at Cardiff University.

00:03:17.189 --> 00:03:20.049
If you want to know more, they have a YouTube
channel you can check out,

00:03:20.049 --> 00:03:21.489
or there's a link in the description.

